00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:48.700
dbSNP just celebrated it's 20th anniversary.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:56.329
It currently has over 680 million reference
snps (RS) from over 2 billion submissions

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:01.959
annotated on both GRCh37 and h38.

00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:06.680
Over half 1 billion RS have frequency data.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:15.020
In addition to mapping to GRCh37 and 38, we
also annotate the RS on the mRNA and protein

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:25.670
and also the functional consequences and ClinVar
clinical significance, allele frequency.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:34.229
You can find out more about the RS and other
attributes that we annotate at the link,

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:35.229
https://go.usa.gov/xpPet.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:42.290
The topic today is on allele frequency so
this is what we currently have in dbSNP . The

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:50.189
name is a misnomer that dbSNP contains both
common and rare variants.

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:56.600
This is a table [Diverse Populations] of the
projects currently dbSNP the Project, the

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.439
number of subjects and the variants that contribute
to each one of those projects.

00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:09.090
DbSNP aggregates data both from the global
survey studies including gnomAD, TopMed, ExAC,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.950
PAGE GO-ESP, 1000 Genomes.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:21.081
Also include regional or cohort specific studies
icluding UK parent and child cohorts (ALSPAC)

00:02:21.081 --> 00:02:22.920
and the twin cohorts (TWINSUK).

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.500
Also we include regional or specific populations
(Estonian, Vietnamese, Northern Sweden) and

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.070
as we identify more of the studies we add
them to dbSNP.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:39.550
And also including the alpha project will
be included in future dbSNP builds.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:46.110
ALFA is on the same scale it is one of the
largest projects in dbSNP.

