00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:54.140
That concludes the dbSNP part of this presentation
next I will describe to you the alpha project

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.440
with dbGaP.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:02.850
dbGaP is a database that is housed at NCBI
that obtains the results of studies that looks

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.540
of the interaction of human genotype and phenotype.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:11.600
It contains a lot of data including over 1.5
million subjects and trillions of genotypes.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.440
So dbGaP actually has two ways to access,
open and controlled.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:22.610
And when it was started in 2007 the Genomic
Summary Results (GSR) that included p-values

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.610
and allelic frequency was initially open access.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:34.089
Shortly after it went live there was a publication
that went out that showed that you can identify

00:08:34.089 --> 00:08:41.559
individuals using statistical methods you
can identify an individual in a study if you

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:43.550
have that person's genome.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:51.019
So in response NIH to protect the participant
and also to evaluate the policy the GSR data

00:08:51.019 --> 00:08:54.430
was moved under controlled access.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:09:02.640
However recently this policy has been reversed
so now we are allowed to again share the summary

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.670
result information includeding allele frequency.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:12.930
We wmbarked on this project called the Allele
Frequency Aggregator or ALFA.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.800
This will aggregate frequency data from dbGaP
and making it available to dbSNP for variant

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.910
interpretation analysis.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:22.519
I will walk you through how we process data.

00:09:22.519 --> 00:09:27.459
This will give you an overview of the processing
pipeline.

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:32.529
All the studies that have been improved for
open access go through QA/QC.

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:37.490
We then compute the ancestry for all the subjects
in the studies.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:43.899
Then we convert all the data into a standard
VCF format Then they get fed into a dbSNP

00:09:43.899 --> 00:09:51.160
build pipeline for normalizing variants that
utilizes a couple of new algorithms and variant

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.639
representations such as SPDI and VOCA.

00:09:54.639 --> 00:09:59.889
You can read about that in the publication
that just came out this month on those algorithms

00:09:59.889 --> 00:10:03.290
we used to normalize the variants (PubMed:
31738401).

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:08.300
Then once we aggregate the variants and normalize
the variants we can aggregate individuals

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:13.869
and genotypes and compute the allele frequency
and make it available to the normal dbSNP

00:10:13.869 --> 00:10:14.869
resources.

00:10:14.869 --> 00:10:23.179
There are a couple of challenges with this
project one of course is the dbGaP large amount

00:10:23.179 --> 00:10:28.089
of data and the other one is harmonizing the
different subjects and populations across

00:10:28.089 --> 00:10:29.089
studies.

00:10:29.089 --> 00:10:35.449
Most of these are self asserted or self-reported
population or they have no ancestry reported.

00:10:35.449 --> 00:10:41.329
The other challenge is of course normalizing
the variants as they come in from different

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:49.149
formats like vcf, plink, they may come from
different assembly versions, called from different

00:10:49.149 --> 00:10:52.120
pipelines and of mixed quality.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:58.929
So to solve the large volume of data and normalizing
variants we used the existing dbSNP and dbGaP

00:10:58.929 --> 00:11:02.139
curations and build pipeline.

00:11:02.139 --> 00:11:12.970
And to normalize the variants we used a program
called GRAF-pop that was developed by dbGaP.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:19.389
What is does is use the Ancestry Informative
Markers (AIMS) to validate the sample ancestry,

00:11:19.389 --> 00:11:24.149
remove duplicates, also to assign subjects
with common ancestry to populations.

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:29.910
I will walk you through briefly how this is
done.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:37.199
This shows you an example of how GRAF-pop
assigns the individual to a particular population.

00:11:37.199 --> 00:11:42.940
What GRAF-pop does is it creates genetic fingerprint
of all the individuals, from the genotype,

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:44.369
and computes genetic distances.

00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:49.600
Then plots them on a graph.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:55.860
What this shows you is a comparison between
what GRAF-pop computed versus what was self-reported

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.320
in the different colors.

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:04.730
Individuals with common ancestry are grouped
together and samples are shown at the vertices.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.920
Then
individuals with mixed ancestry will fall

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.690
along the axes.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:17.009
Shown in blue ring is the 95 percent interval,
those are a common ancestry for the individuals

00:12:17.009 --> 00:12:21.160
we assigned to the populations the vertices,
African, European or East Asian.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.949
Here are subjects with mixed ancestry.

00:12:23.949 --> 00:12:32.579
For example here, there are subjects reported
as Hispanic but GRAF-pop identified as two

00:12:32.579 --> 00:12:34.519
separate ancestries.

00:12:34.519 --> 00:12:42.329
One is a Hispanic group with an Afro Caribbean
ancestry, and one Hispanic group that has

00:12:42.329 --> 00:12:43.769
more European ancestry.

00:12:43.769 --> 00:12:49.389
So that is how we assigned the individuals
to the different populations and there are

00:12:49.389 --> 00:12:53.389
12 different populations that GRAF-pop assigned
them to.

00:12:53.389 --> 00:12:59.910
Any individual of mixed ancestry not within
the 95th percentile are assigned to Other.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:08.079
We also use the AIM markers to QC the variants
and the allele frequency reported by each

00:13:08.079 --> 00:13:09.079
study.

00:13:09.079 --> 00:13:15.259
We compared the markers from the studies and
then compared them to the frequency reported

00:13:15.259 --> 00:13:16.459
by 1000 Genomes.

00:13:16.459 --> 00:13:22.999
This is a plot of the variant allele frequency
in the study versus 1000 Genomes.

00:13:22.999 --> 00:13:27.739
Of course, this is a nice... they are in concordance,
so that is good.

00:13:27.739 --> 00:13:30.809
At least for these European subjects.

00:13:30.809 --> 00:13:37.059
This shows you that this study did not pass
QC because more than half of the variants

00:13:37.059 --> 00:13:41.100
are reported where the allele had be flipped
that's why you see this the axes have been

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.350
inverted essentially.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:45.529
Shown in red here.

00:13:45.529 --> 00:13:52.959
Then more subtle issues that we identified
here where there seems to be some systematic

00:13:52.959 --> 00:13:58.660
error where you expect some variants to be
random are assigned at 0.3 or 0.7 so that

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.869
failed QC also.

00:14:00.869 --> 00:14:08.069
Then even more subtle is where 1000 Genomes
reported that some of the markers are heterozygous,

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:12.389
but the dbGaP study reported some as homozygous.

00:14:12.389 --> 00:14:19.600
Typically they are derived from micro-array
assay at probably these are probes that failed

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:24.129
in the assay failed to detect the heterozygosity.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:30.179
So that illustrates kind of the process and
the steps that we go for the QC for the subjects

00:14:30.179 --> 00:14:32.049
and for the variants.

00:14:32.049 --> 00:14:41.439
Once we have done all of the QC, as I mentioned
before we convert the data to a standard format

00:14:41.439 --> 00:14:46.850
and that goes into the dbGaP pipeline where
they get normalized and assigned RS numbers

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.379
if they are novel, or get assigned to existing
RS numbers they are assigned to an existing

00:14:50.379 --> 00:14:55.549
RS number then distributed to the usual product
pipeline.

00:14:55.549 --> 00:15:01.449
This is a build summary we did recently of
about 50 studies from dbGaP that have been

00:15:01.449 --> 00:15:11.309
approved for open access and that is comprised
of over 140,000 objects and over half 1 trillion

00:15:11.309 --> 00:15:12.309
genotypes.

00:15:12.309 --> 00:15:20.439
And these are normalized or aggregated to
almost half 1 billion RefSNP.

00:15:20.439 --> 00:15:26.370
The majority of them already exist in dbSNP
and about 20 million that are novel.

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:32.490
This is a breakdown of the populations from
that build.

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:39.070
Most of it is European 75% European there
is some representation of the other continental

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.920
populations.

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:47.220
This only represents 10% of the potential
1.5 million subjects in dbGaP so far and we

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:55.120
will be adding our goal is to increase underrepresented
populations in the future builds and releases.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:59.399
That may include new cohorts and new populations.

00:15:59.399 --> 00:16:05.319
They will be included with the quarterly dbSNP
builds.

00:16:05.319 --> 00:16:11.519
The data for ALFA will be reported on the
RefSNP web page just as I showed before for

00:16:11.519 --> 00:16:15.379
existing data for other projects.

00:16:15.379 --> 00:16:20.339
The data will also be accessible by API.

00:16:20.339 --> 00:16:25.420
You can get the metadata for the populations
and the studies.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:31.040
you can also query by position range and also
by RS numbers.

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:39.759
Of course the data will also be available
for download from the FTP site.

00:16:39.759 --> 00:16:50.920
It is a standard vcf format: accession, position,
rs ID, alleles, then there will be a column

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.749
for each of the populations with the allele
counts.

00:16:54.749 --> 00:17:01.819
I mentioned before the first release of the
alpha data will be coming out in January 2020

00:17:01.819 --> 00:17:03.709
so look for that announcement.

00:17:03.709 --> 00:17:10.569
With that we will move on to the demo section
