00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:17.860
First I will demonstrate the Entrez search
then we will go into the API.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:25.779
You can start from dbSNP homepage I'm going
to use use the example on our help page if

00:17:25.779 --> 00:17:35.110
you go here like I mentioned before there
are additional search terms supported there

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:37.620
are some examples shown here to some complex
query.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.241
I am going to look at this first example which
is kind of interesting, how you can use the

00:17:41.241 --> 00:17:45.510
global minor allele frequency to interrogate
whether an allele is suspect or not.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:51.429
I'll click on this then explain it in a bit.

00:17:51.429 --> 00:17:57.529
Typically what I like to do is look at the
outliers so look for variants where the frequency

00:17:57.529 --> 00:18:03.750
is not detected in any of the projects or
that's rare.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.460
So here I am creating where the frequencies
are zero.

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:15.159
What this shows is that suggests that there
may be an assembly bias and that the assembly

00:18:15.159 --> 00:18:20.590
may have been incorrectly annotated at that
position so that when compared against what

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:23.789
the assembly would call a variant there but
then they go and genotype it will fail and

00:18:23.789 --> 00:18:27.130
they will not detect heterozygosity or allele
frequency.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:32.710
For instance if you look at the first one
here you can see on GRCh38

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:36.240
the position reported, the allele, is C-G.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.409
And then if you look at the allele frequency
everybody is non-detectable, the variant allele

00:18:41.409 --> 00:18:42.659
is non-detectable.

00:18:42.659 --> 00:18:49.809
But then if you look at the HGVS we report
on both GRCh37 and 38, this is 38 for this

00:18:49.809 --> 00:18:52.279
one you see the C to G here.

00:18:52.279 --> 00:18:58.100
If you look at GRCh37 you see the alleles
have flipped.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:06.919
Then if you interrogate further, look at the
dbSNP web page you see no evidence that the

00:19:06.919 --> 00:19:12.460
variants allele -- whether it is an artifact
or real.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:17.549
So if you look here, here is GRCh37 and 38
you see the allele is flipped.

00:19:17.549 --> 00:19:23.190
But then if you go down to frequency -- you
can also see the evidence for this by using

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:28.940
the sequence viewer by looking up -- adding
the alignment tracks for GRCh37 and 38.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:37.120
Go to Configure Tracks and then go to Sequence
then scroll all the way down we have alignment

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:47.299
for GRCh38 and all of the alternate assemblies
and the older versions of the GRC assemblies.

00:19:47.299 --> 00:19:56.590
Go all the way down to GRCh37, the alignment
between 38 and 37 and add that.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:20:01.519
There you can see a mismatch between GRCh37
and 38.

00:20:01.519 --> 00:20:06.140
So this is an example of how you can use allele
frequency, it is probably not a normal use

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:10.350
case but it is kind of interesting.

